In Figure 6.34, we show our blast-wave results along with other experiments 3. The results are tabulated in Table C.20. One can see that both \(T_{\rm kin}\) and \(\langle\beta_{T}\rangle\) rapidly increase at low energy.

Footnote 3: Aside from the difference in centrality cuts (top 5% for EOS, top 1% for FOPI), EOS analyzed \(p\), \(d\), \(t\), \({}^{3}He\), and \(\alpha\), while FOPI looked at heavier fragments (\(Z\leq 15\)). This is probably the reason for the distinct difference between \(\langle beta_{T}\rangle\) between EOS and FOPI.

The temperature shows a sign of saturation around the SPS, or even the AGS, energies. The saturation of \(T_{\rm kin}\) seems consistent with the notion that the freeze-out temperature has to be colder than the predicted transition temperature (\(T_{\rm crit}\)). These plateau structures may suggest that the system is going through a phase transition. At a phase transition of matter, some state observables stay constant, such as the temperature of water while boiling. The flow velocity keeps increasing with the energy. RHIC flow velocity at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=200\) GeV is approximately three times that at the AGS (\(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=2\) GeV). The strong radial flow indicates that we have a dense system of particles that scatter frequently. Although the fact that \(T_{\rm kin}\) and \(\langle\beta_{T}\rangle\) can explain the data is not a sufficient evidence of thermalization, it supports such notion.

